              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Transformations**\n\nOkay, I'm trying to wrap my head around this grid transformation. It seems like the core idea is about changing 0s to 1s, which is cool. I'm focusing on the existing 1s and how they fit in. Are they guiding the process, or do they serve as starting points for something? Example 1 is on my mind, I'm checking it for 1s placement.\n\n\n**Investigating Zero Connectivity**\n\nOkay, so I've zeroed in on the connectivity aspect. It's looking like the transformation hinges on identifying which zero cells are directly or indirectly linked to existing ones. I'm seeing a flood-fill behavior, where ones spread through adjacent zeros. Example 1 is really helping solidify this idea. I'm focusing on why some zeros stay zero, and whether that's because they are isolated from all ones.\n\n\n**Examining Adjacency Rules**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the adjacency rules that govern the transformation of zeros to ones. I'm specifically looking at why (1,6) in the example remains a zero, despite being surrounded by non-zero neighbors. It seems like the surrounding non-zero values are key in determining if a zero becomes a one. I'm tracing the propagation to see why certain zeros remain isolated. I'm investigating how the input values themselves affect the adjacency, and which zeros do not change. I've found an interesting case at (4,5) and (5,4) where the values do not transform, and are surrounded by zeros, which is interesting to me.\n\n\n**Defining Connectivity Criterion**\n\nI've been refining the connectivity rule. It looks like any zero cell 4-connected to an initial one, either directly or through a path of zeros, becomes a one. I'm verifying that this rule accurately transforms the grid. I am checking the connectivity of zeros to the set of initial ones. It appears the transformation only propagates through the zeros, which is a key observation. The edge-sharing rule, or 4-connectivity, seems central.\n\n\n**Tracing Propagation Path**\n\nI'm now tracing the propagation path from a specific cell, seeing how ones spread. I see the importance of a 1 at (3,8) and how that leads to (4,7) becoming a 1, going up and across. I'm focusing on why a zero becomes a one, specifically looking at how connectivity to initial ones is the key driver. I'm noting specific instances and the flow.\n\n\n",
